From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:38:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: Move the shared authenticator to the new first link

Since commit a213fee11da3 ("AP: MLO: Make IEEE 802.1X SM, authserv, and
RADIUS client singletons"), the links of an AP MLD share the IEEE 802.1X
authenticator, the authentication server and the RADIUS client of the
first BSS. These objects keep pointers to that BSS:
- the context of the authenticator callbacks and its ERP keys,
- the WPS context, message context and IMSI privacy key of the EAP
  server configuration,
- the context of the RADIUS client, of its IEEE 802.1X handler and of
  its interim accounting error callback,
- the context of RADIUS DAS, of the EAP-SIM database and of the RADIUS
  server.
They also keep pointers into the configuration of that BSS. The RADIUS
client and its pending messages use its RADIUS server list, and the
RADIUS server uses its erp_domain string.

When the first link is removed, hostapd_mld_remove_link() makes the
next link the first BSS. The removed BSS drops only its own pointers,
and its data and configuration are freed. The next RADIUS message of
another link reads the freed server list, and the next state machine
step reads the freed BSS. With accounting enabled, hostapd crashes when
another link is removed later:

  WPA_TRACE: eloop SIGSEGV - START
  MD5_Update()
  radius_msg_finish_acct() ../src/radius/radius.c:581
  radius_client_send() ../src/radius/radius_client.c:956
  accounting_report_state() ../src/ap/accounting.c:455
  accounting_deinit() ../src/ap/accounting.c:547
  hostapd_free_hapd_data() ../src/ap/hostapd.c:547

Move all of these to the new first BSS when the first BSS changes. The
RADIUS server list moves with the client, as the VLAN list does in
hostapd_mld_move_vlan_list(). The RADIUS server keeps its own copy of
erp_domain, as it does for t_c_server_url. The other links take the
pointer to the RADIUS server as well, as they do for the EAP server
configuration, so that the server can move. A configuration reload of a
link other than the first leaves the shared RADIUS client alone, since
that configuration goes away with the link.

Each link also registers its ACL and accounting handlers on the shared
RADIUS client, and nothing removes them when the link goes away. A
RADIUS reply then reaches the ACL handler of a removed link, which reads
its freed query list, and a disabled link adds a second set of handlers
when it is enabled again. Remove the handlers of a BSS at its deinit.

The client has one interim accounting error callback, and each link
replaces its context with its own BSS at setup. The callback then finds
only the stations of the link that was set up last, and keeps that BSS
after its removal. Register the callback with the first BSS, move it
with the other contexts, and let it prefer the station entry whose
interim accounting timer is pending.

The EAP server configuration also holds the eap_server setting of the
first BSS. The setting is a per-BSS parameter and can differ between
the links, for example where WPS, which needs the internal EAP server,
runs on some links only. After the move, the configuration keeps the
setting of the removed BSS. Where that BSS had eap_server=0 and the new
first BSS has eap_server=1, eap_sm_Policy_getDecision() picks the
passthrough mode after the EAP-Response/Identity of a station of the
new first BSS and of an MLO station. The response goes to the RADIUS
client, and a WPS Enrollee on the new first link never gets an EAP-WSC
exchange.

Take the eap_server setting of the new first BSS as well. An MLO
station, whose one session serves all its links, then follows the mode
of the new first link, as it followed the mode of the first link before
the move. Keep the setting where the links share a RADIUS server, since
radius_server_init() enables the internal EAP server in the shared
configuration for the sessions of that server.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
